Item 10 is done and the gate passes on the final state. Item 11, the reserve, is still open, so I did not add `LOOP_COMPLETE`.

**The second review** is in `paper2/review_readability_2.md`. I read the 37-page PDF from start to finish, as text and as rendered pages. All ten changes the first review asked for are now in the paper. The central question is on p. 2 and in the abstract, and Section 6 answers it. The counts of exact, band and false rows agree everywhere. No project-internal wording is left in the body, and Figure 1.1, Algorithm 4.1 and the experimental setup are all present. Writing the three-sentence summary took no searching, and the four places where the first reviewer got stuck now read clearly.

**I fixed the five worst new problems.** These are wording and notation changes only; no number, theorem or claim changed.
1. Theorem 2.6(1) and Table 2.3 used `|N|`, which is never defined and clashes with the neighbourhood `N[c]`. It now reads `|C|`, the number of nets.
2. The conclusion says the dataset serves "nine problems", but the exact core has ten names. Section 5.4 now says multiple folding equals gate matrix layout, giving "nine problems in all".
3. Section 5.1 said the open-stacks benchmarks came from "five collections" and then named four. It now says four, one of which is distributed twice.
4. Table 5.3's caption ranked the open collections by share without saying so, and left out Rome, which has 327 open classes (more than the VSPLIB grids and Harwell–Boeing together). The caption now names it.
5. The introduction said "the proofs are given in the text", but Section 2 cites the published proofs for the exact core instead of repeating them. It now says "given or cited in the text".

I also renamed Appendix A's entry A.39 from "implementation faults" to the better move's "two requirements", to match how Section 4.4 now describes them. The page count is still 37; my first wording of fix 5 pushed it to 38, so I shortened it.

**Still open:**
- **Length:** the body grew from about 22 to 25 pages, because the additions the first review asked for outweighed the moves to appendices. If it has to shrink, Section 4 is the place to cut.
- **Title:** it is now just "The pathwidth complex". A subtitle naming open stacks would help IJOC readers; that is your call.
- **Small items for the reserve item:** five one-sentence problems are listed in the review's §5, items 7–11.

**The long run:** it is still running, with no `!!!` line in its log. The two never-certified values were still partial at about 22:00. The paper still prints the counts read at 20:21 the same day, under "as of 6 October 2026", so §5.2 and Table B.1 should be refreshed on the day of submission.

